    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75412.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "You're listening to a podcast from the BBC World Service. This is hard talk with me, Stephen Sacker. Thanks for downloading this edition of the program. I do hope you enjoy it. My guest today has a personal biography that evokes the power of the American dream. Marco Rubio's parents were born poor in Cuba. They migrated to the US and worked in blue-collar jobs to ensure their kids had a future better than theirs. Their son Marco studied hard. He became a lawyer and then a Florida politician. By 40, he'd already won a seat in the US Senate. He was the darling of the so-called Tea Party movement of root and branch conservatives. He seemed to be in America's political sweet spot, a conservative but also an Hispanic American with an appeal far beyond the Republican core white constituency. At least that was the theory. In the past year, Republicans in Congress have been locked in a political war of attrition with the Obama White House over the federal budget, healthcare and immigration. The public has been turned off. Senator Rubio's poll ratings have suffered, and amid the speculation about a run for the White House, he faces an awkward question. Is he the kind of Republican that can win a national election? Well, Marco Rubio joins me now. Welcome to Hard Talk.",
