    "turn_text": "huge proportion of the American? Well, there were two things in debate on that issue. There was the budget, the short-term budget extension, which was what was tied to a debate over Obamacare. And the question at the time was, we are prepared to fund the entire government, and we made proposals to do so. In fact, the U.S. House of Representatives passed a budget that funded the government. It simply did not fund Obamacare. And I think in retrospect, you can look at that tactically, whether it was a wise decision or not. I think most people are beginning to conclude it was not a tactical decision that was wise. The secondary debate about the debt limit, I think, is actually a much more serious one because we have entitlement programs in the U.S. that are simply unsustainable, is currently structured, and years are going by with nothing being done to address it. And I have a personal frustration that, quite frankly, neither party up until this point has been serious about addressing what I think is a very serious threat to our economic well-being over the long term.",
