    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "But that's the fundamental issue to divide the country. What do you do with 10 or 11 million people living in your country who have violated the immigration laws? And I think that the vast majority of people recognize that you cannot round up and deport 11 million people. The question now becomes, what can you do to address that? And I think we've put forth an idea. that there's real consequences for violating the laws. You're going to have to wait 10 years, you're going to have to undergo background checks, you're going to have to pay a fine, but ultimately you're going to have the opportunity to access legal residency. The problem is that there is a real lack of trust that the federal government will ever enforce the laws. In essence, it has become difficult to convince people, not just Republicans. It's become difficult to convince people that these 11 million people, if you legalize them, will not be quickly followed by 11 million more in a decade or less. And that's the problem that we're facing, is that. So what I've argued is, let's begin to move on the things we agree on in immigration so that we can make some progress and create some momentum to finish the",
