  {
    "episode_id": 75412,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75412.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 61,
    "turn_text": "You've asked two separate questions. On the anti-poverty programs, I'm a supporter of the safety net, not as a way of life, as a way of helping people who have fallen to get back on their feet and try again, and as a way of helping those who cannot help themselves. I think it's important for our safety net programs to encourage work, to encourage independence, and not to reward dependence on government, and so forth. And even Democrats have been supportive of reforms to welfare. For example, Bill Clinton was involved in welfare reform. On the issue of abortion, I do not analyze that issue from a political perspective. It is an issue that I hold deeply. I respect, I do not underestimate, the difficulty that a young woman faces when confronted with a pregnancy she didn't want and didn't plan. That being said, I fundamentally believe that every human being has a right to live. I fundamentally believe that a human life is entitled to the protection of our law. And to me, that's a deeply held belief. It's not a political one. If there are political consequences for that belief, I'm prepared to accept them. Because again, for me, it's not about denying anyone the right to choose. It's about protecting a human being's right to live.",
