    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/5/75412.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 105,
    "turn_text": "first of all, I haven't made a determination whether I'm running for that office or even if I'm running for reelection to the Senate. I don't know who the next nominee, the Republican Party, is going to be. But you asked an important question, and that was, do I think that the political fallout of immigration reform is something that I think will impede my ability to run for reelection or another office? And the answer is, I'm not sure. But the bottom line is that that's not the reason why I make decisions. I'm going to always try to make decisions based on what I think is the right thing. I'm going to go to the people whom I represent and explain to them why it is I've taken on this issue, why I think it's the right approach, and hope to convince them. Sometimes that will work and sometimes that will not. But at the end of the day, I want to make sure that I can look back at my time in public service. and say, I did what was right even though it wasn't always popular. I did what was right even if it came at political expense. It's interesting that we live in a time when some of the criticisms of politics is that we're not addressing the major issues of our time. That requires you often to take political risk. And I'm prepared to do that in order to achieve positive results for my country.",
